//! lotus/normalize.rye — ALES103: peak normalization — measure the loudest sample through ALES13's meter, then scale the WHOLE clip through ALES2's gain so that
//! loudest sample lands exactly on a chosen target. This is the single most-reached-for gesture in every editor and DAW: the "Normalize" button. A take recorded quiet
//! and a take recorded hot both leave the same peak after normalize, so a keeper mixes without fighting the input level. It amplifies a quiet clip (peak below target)
//! and attenuates a hot one (peak above target) with the SAME arithmetic — one fraction, target/peak, applied to every sample.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: measure the clip's peak magnitude through ALES13's `measure`; a silent clip (peak 0) has no peak to lift and is left EXACTLY as it is (no
//! divide by zero); otherwise scale the whole clip `[0, clip.len)` by target/peak through ALES2's `gain`. The peak sample, at magnitude `peak`, maps to exactly ±target
//! (a division that is exact for that sample), and every other sample, being strictly quieter, maps strictly inside ±target — so the NEW peak equals `target` exactly.
//!
//! The crux — four laws. THE SCALE LAW: normalize is nothing but ALES13's `measure` followed by ALES2's `gain(clip, 0, len, target, peak)` — it invents no new arithmetic,
//! so it can never drift from the meter it reads or the gain it delegates to. THE PEAK LAW: after normalize to `target`, the clip's peak magnitude equals `target` exactly
//! (for any non-silent clip, at any target) — the loudest sample lands on the target and nothing overshoots. THE SILENCE LAW: an all-zero clip (and an empty clip) is
//! unchanged whatever the target — no peak, no gain, no fault. THE IDEMPOTENCE LAW: normalizing to a target, then again to the same target, is a no-op the second time
//! (the peak already sits on the target, so the second gain is unity) — normalize is settled once it lands.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus · the creative suite) · waymark ALES · rung ALES103.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260815-001849_fill-ales103-lotus-peak-normalize.md
//! Stands on: lotus/meter.rye (ALES13) — the peak/RMS meter, its `measure` and `peak` read the loudest sample read-only; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) — the Clip and the
//! saturating `gain` that scales it; lotus/power.rye (ALES11) — the isqrt the meter is built on, reached only through ALES13.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL. A bounded in-process buffer of i16 PCM on one bench, siloed to lotus/. It changes only sample VALUES through ALES2's own
//! gain, never a length, never a byte fabricated; the target is a plain magnitude in samples, NOT a decibel level (no real reference level exists yet — that conversion
//! is a later rung), and no loudness model (K-weighting, LUFS) is claimed — this is PEAK normalization, the plainest reading. No real sample rate, no network, no keys,
//! no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/normalize.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const meter = @import("meter.rye");

pub const normalize_version = "20260815.001849";

/// Peak normalization's faults are the meter's own `MeterError` (only the honest-unreachable MeterFull — a Clip is bounded well inside the meter's window) and the
/// editor's own `EditError` (from `gain`, only the honest-unreachable BadGain/BadRange — the denominator is a measured non-zero peak and the span is the whole clip),
/// reused by name, plus one of its own: `BadTarget` for a target that is zero (not a normalize) or past the positive full-scale ceiling (unreachable by any sample).
pub const NormalizeError = meter.MeterError || timeline.EditError || error{
    /// A target of zero (normalizing to silence is not a normalize) or past `sample_max` (no positive sample can reach it) — refused before any read or write.
    BadTarget,
};

/// Scale `clip` so its loudest sample lands exactly on `target` (a magnitude in [1, sample_max]). Measures the peak through ALES13's `measure`, then scales the whole
/// clip by target/peak through ALES2's `gain` — amplifying a quiet clip and attenuating a hot one with the same fraction. A silent clip (peak 0) is left exactly as it
/// is; a zero or over-ceiling target refuses `BadTarget`, the clip untouched.
pub fn normalize_peak(clip: *timeline.Clip, target: u32) NormalizeError!void {
    // invariant: the target is a reachable positive magnitude — zero is not a normalize, and no positive i16 sample can exceed sample_max, so a larger target is a lie.
    if (target == 0 or target > @as(u32, @intCast(timeline.sample_max))) return error.BadTarget;

    const original_len: u32 = clip.len;

    // Measure the loudest sample read-only through ALES13 — all of the meter's own bounds run here before a single sample is scaled.
    const m = try meter.measure(clip);
    const peak_mag: u32 = meter.peak(&m);

    // invariant: the meter read exactly this clip's samples, and its peak is a legal magnitude within the meter's domain.
    assert(m.count == original_len and peak_mag <= meter.sample_peak_max);

    // A silent clip has no peak to lift — leaving it exactly as it is is the only honest normalize, and it keeps the division below from ever dividing by zero.
    if (peak_mag == 0) {
        // invariant: the silent path changes nothing — same length, same samples.
        assert(clip.len == original_len);
        return;
    }

    // Scale the whole clip by target/peak through ALES2's saturating gain — the peak sample maps to exactly ±target, every quieter sample strictly inside it. The
    // denominator is the measured non-zero peak (no BadGain) and the span is the whole clip (no BadRange), so the forwarded EditError is honestly unreachable here.
    try timeline.gain(clip, 0, original_len, @intCast(target), peak_mag);

    // invariant: normalize changes sample values only — the length and bound are exactly what they were.
    assert(clip.len == original_len);
    assert(clip.len <= clip.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE SCALE LAW — normalize equals measure-then-gain on a known peak (a ×2 amplify and a ×0.5 attenuate land exact values); THE PEAK LAW — after normalize
//     to a target, the re-measured peak equals the target exactly across many clips and targets, including the i16-minimum peak at the domain ceiling; THE SILENCE LAW —
//     an all-zero clip and an empty clip are unchanged whatever the target; THE IDEMPOTENCE LAW — normalize twice to one target equals once; plus amplify-vs-attenuate
//     direction and the two BadTarget refusals leaving the clip untouched ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_clip(clip: *timeline.Clip, src: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(clip, 0, src);
}

/// The clip's current peak magnitude, re-measured through ALES13 — the reader the peak law checks the result against.
fn clip_peak(clip: *const timeline.Clip) !u32 {
    const m = try meter.measure(clip);
    return meter.peak(&m);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE SCALE LAW — normalize is measure-then-gain, so a known peak lands known values. A clip whose peak is 8000, normalized to 16000, is scaled ×2 exactly; a clip
    //    whose peak is 30000, normalized to 15000, is scaled ×0.5 exactly. divTrunc rounds toward zero, the plain gain rule.
    {
        var up = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&up, &[_]i16{ 4000, -8000, 2000 }); // peak 8000
        try normalize_peak(&up, 16000);
        if (!eq(up.samples(), &[_]i16{ 8000, -16000, 4000 })) return error.ScaleUpWrong; // ×2

        var down = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&down, &[_]i16{ 20000, -30000, 10000 }); // peak 30000
        try normalize_peak(&down, 15000);
        if (!eq(down.samples(), &[_]i16{ 10000, -15000, 5000 })) return error.ScaleDownWrong; // ×0.5
    }

    // 2. THE PEAK LAW — after normalize to a target, the re-measured peak equals the target EXACTLY, for every non-silent clip at every target. The loudest sample lands
    //    on the target and nothing overshoots (each quieter sample maps strictly inside ±target).
    {
        const clips = [_][]const i16{
            &[_]i16{ 4000, -8000, 2000 },
            &[_]i16{ 20000, -30000, 10000 },
            &[_]i16{ 100, 0, -50, 25 },
            &[_]i16{ -32768, 100, -200, 300 }, // peak magnitude 32768 — the i16 minimum at the meter's domain ceiling
            &[_]i16{ 1, -1, 1, -1 },
        };
        const targets = [_]u32{ 1, 100, 1000, 16000, 32767 };
        var ci: u32 = 0;
        while (ci < clips.len) : (ci += 1) {
            var ti: u32 = 0;
            while (ti < targets.len) : (ti += 1) {
                var clip = timeline.Clip{};
                try load_clip(&clip, clips[ci]);
                try normalize_peak(&clip, targets[ti]);
                if (try clip_peak(&clip) != targets[ti]) return error.PeakNotTarget;
            }
        }
    }

    // 3. THE SILENCE LAW — an all-zero clip is unchanged whatever the target (peak 0, no gain, no divide by zero), and an empty clip likewise (nothing to measure).
    {
        const targets = [_]u32{ 1, 1000, 32767 };
        var ti: u32 = 0;
        while (ti < targets.len) : (ti += 1) {
            var zeros = timeline.Clip{};
            try load_clip(&zeros, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 });
            try normalize_peak(&zeros, targets[ti]);
            if (!eq(zeros.samples(), &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0 })) return error.SilenceChanged;

            var empty = timeline.Clip{};
            try normalize_peak(&empty, targets[ti]);
            if (empty.len != 0) return error.EmptyChanged;
        }
    }

    // 4. THE IDEMPOTENCE LAW — normalize to a target, then again to the same target, is a no-op the second time: the peak already sits on the target, so the second gain
    //    is unity and every sample maps to itself.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ 4000, -8000, 2000, -1000 });
        try normalize_peak(&clip, 20000);
        var once = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&once, clip.samples());
        try normalize_peak(&clip, 20000); // second pass
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), once.samples())) return error.NotIdempotent;
    }

    // 5. AMPLIFY vs ATTENUATE — a clip quieter than the target gets louder (its peak rises to the target), a clip louder than the target gets quieter (its peak falls to
    //    the target). Two directions, one fraction.
    {
        var quiet = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&quiet, &[_]i16{ 1000, -2000, 500 }); // peak 2000, below target
        const before_q = try clip_peak(&quiet);
        try normalize_peak(&quiet, 20000);
        const after_q = try clip_peak(&quiet);
        if (!(after_q > before_q and after_q == 20000)) return error.AmplifyWrong;

        var hot = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&hot, &[_]i16{ 25000, -30000, 12000 }); // peak 30000, above target
        const before_h = try clip_peak(&hot);
        try normalize_peak(&hot, 5000);
        const after_h = try clip_peak(&hot);
        if (!(after_h < before_h and after_h == 5000)) return error.AttenuateWrong;
    }

    // 6a. A ZERO TARGET refuses BadTarget — normalizing to silence is not a normalize; the clip is untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300 });
        if (normalize_peak(&clip, 0)) |_| return error.ZeroTargetSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadTarget) return error.WrongZeroTarget;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300 })) return error.ZeroTargetMutated;
    }

    // 6b. A TARGET PAST sample_max refuses BadTarget — no positive sample can reach it, so it is a lie; the clip is untouched.
    {
        var clip = timeline.Clip{};
        try load_clip(&clip, &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300 });
        if (normalize_peak(&clip, 32768)) |_| return error.OverTargetSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadTarget) return error.WrongOverTarget;
        if (!eq(clip.samples(), &[_]i16{ 100, -200, 300 })) return error.OverTargetMutated;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-normalize: PEAK NORMALIZATION — measure the loudest sample through ALES13's meter, then scale the WHOLE clip through ALES2's gain so that sample " ++
        "lands exactly on a chosen target (the Normalize button every editor ships). The rule: measure the peak; a silent clip is left exactly as it is (no divide by " ++
        "zero); else scale by target/peak, the peak sample mapping to exactly ±target and every quieter sample strictly inside it. The crux is four laws. THE SCALE " ++
        "LAW: normalize is measure-then-gain and invents no arithmetic, so a peak of 8000 to 16000 is a clean ×2 and a peak of 30000 to 15000 a clean ×0.5. THE PEAK " ++
        "LAW: after normalize the re-measured peak equals the target EXACTLY across many clips and targets, including the i16 minimum at the domain ceiling. THE " ++
        "SILENCE LAW: an all-zero clip and an empty clip are unchanged whatever the target. THE IDEMPOTENCE LAW: normalize twice to one target equals once. Amplify " ++
        "raises a quiet clip's peak to the target and attenuate lowers a hot clip's; a zero target and a target past sample_max each refuse BadTarget, the clip " ++
        "untouched. Purely local — it changes sample values only through gain, fabricating none; a plain sample magnitude, NOT a decibel or a loudness model; no real " ++
        "sample rate, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
